  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Heliot Ramos drove in three runs while Daniel Susac's eighth-inning home run sparked a late rally as the Giants overwhelmed Milwaukee on Wednesday, 16\u20133.",
    "what_this_means": "They led from the second inning onward and never trailed. Webb pitched six solid innings while the lineup converted eleven of twenty-two runners in scoring position across four separate multi-run innings. This was less a comeback than a methodical breakout. The offense built a lead early and then expanded it late. Ramos kept the production flowing when it mattered most, but this game belonged to the lineup as a whole. Seventeen hits and five strikeouts tells you everything about who showed up today. The bullpen. Hentges, Kilian, and Winn combining for three scoreless innings. Merely held what was already won. When the offense is working, everything else becomes quiet noise. This is what balanced production looks like for a team that has lived on inconsistency all season.",
    "what_to_watch": "San Diego brought a .234 team batting average to the ballpark tonight. That matchup is one the Giants should exploit early. JP Sears starts for the Padres with a 3.86 ERA, but the Giants have shown they can hit right-handed pitching when they're locked in like they were today.",
